

WHO(1)              UNIX Programmer's Manual                 WHO(1)



NAME
     who - who is on the system

SYNOPSIS
     who [ who-file ]
     who am i

DESCRIPTION
     _W_h_o, without an argument, lists the login name, terminal name
     and login time for each current user of the system.

     _W_h_o with one argument reads the named file instead of
     /etc/utmp, so that `who /etc/utmp' is the default and any
     file of the same record format may be examined.

     _W_h_o with two arguments, as in `who am i' (and also `who are
     you'), prints the invoker's own line prefixed by the host
     name and a `!'.

FILES
     /etc/utmp     the login accounting file
     /etc/hostname the host name, for `who am i'

SEE ALSO
     w(1), getty(8), login(1), utmp(5)

BUGS
     A login name longer than eight characters is truncated in the
     accounting record and therefore here; see utmp(5).

     The host field is always empty, no login here arrives over a
     network with a name to record, so the parenthesised remote
     host never prints.

FGX                            2.11BSD                           1
